    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. Add more faulty qubits and the logical qubit can become more reliable. That sounds backward. It works only below a code-and-noise-dependent threshold, with fault-tolerant control.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Explain why a larger quantum code can fail less often.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — FAULT TOLERANCE",
            "segment": "Below the Threshold",
            "greeting": "Hej, Liam",
            "runningText": "comparing physical and logical error…",
            "output": [
              "Below threshold: larger distance suppresses logical failure.",
              "Above threshold: added hardware does not buy suppression."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "A logical qubit is encoded across many physical degrees of freedom. The code can tolerate some error patterns before a logical failure occurs.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Code distance measures how large an undetectable logical error must be. Increasing distance raises the minimum weight of that damaging pattern, while also adding qubits and operations.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "At low physical error rate, damaging high-weight patterns become rapidly less likely. The extra protection wins over the extra places where faults can occur.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Plot logical error against physical error for several code distances. The curves cross near a threshold region. Below it, larger distance pushes the curve downward.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "split"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Above that region, increasing distance does not suppress logical error. Extra faulty components and correction circuitry can outweigh the code's added protection.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The threshold is not a universal percentage. It depends on the code, decoder, gate set, measurement schedule, geometry, and the noise model.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Correlated faults, leakage, drift, and long-range crosstalk can violate simple noise assumptions. A reported component error below some number is not by itself proof of scalable fault tolerance.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "threshold"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Experiments can test below-threshold behavior by comparing logical error across increasing code sizes under matched operating conditions. The signature is systematic suppression as distance grows.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Even below threshold, useful logical error rates require overhead. More physical qubits, repeated syndrome cycles, decoding, and carefully fault-tolerant logical operations are still needed.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:23:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. Three larger codes show lower logical error only on the low-noise side of a crossing. What does the crossing mean? Pause before the answer: an empirical threshold region for that code, decoder, circuit, and noise environment.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
